course: dictionary
lesson: 9099
title: Slyly and how words show the way
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Slyly» is an adverb, a describing word that tells you how something is done. «When the Whale says he is still hungry, the little fish slyly asks whether he has ever tasted Man.» The fish asks a question, and slyly tells you the way he asks it.
explain: Slyly means in a clever, secret way, often to trick someone. Look at the clues: the Whale says he is hungry, and the little fish asks a sneaky question. He is not asking honestly. He is up to something clever.
explain: Slyly can also mean in a playful, knowing way, as if sharing a secret joke. «She smiled slyly.» Nobody is being tricked here. Her smile shows she knows something the others don't.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does the action sound tricky, or does it sound playful? The words around slyly tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Slyly is an adverb: it tells you how something is done.
fact: «Slyly» means in a clever, secret way, often to trick someone.
fact: «Slyly» can also mean in a playful, knowing way, as if sharing a secret joke.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, the weasels and stoats slyly take over Toad Hall.
fact: The spelling «slily» also exists, but it is rare. «Slyly» is the usual spelling.
exercise: «When the Whale says he is still hungry, the little fish slyly asks whether he has ever tasted Man.» What does slyly tell us about the way the little fish asks? | *Clever and secret, as if up to a trick | Angry and cross | Happy and loud | Very tired
exercise: «Mia slyly winked at me, as if we shared a secret joke.» What does slyly mean here? | Loudly and crossly | Quickly and without thinking | Without any sound at all | *In a playful, knowing way
exercise: «The weasels slyly took over Toad Hall.» Which word is the adverb? | Hall | took | weasels | *slyly
test: Foxes slyly steal the hens while the farmer sleeps. What does slyly suggest about the foxes? | Being loud and cheerful | Being sleepy and slow | *Being clever and secret, often to trick someone | Being hungry and sad
test: «Mia slyly winked at me, as if we shared a secret joke.» Which meaning fits best? | Quickly | *In a playful, knowing way | Sadly | Angrily
exam: Which meaning fits «The weasels slyly took over Toad Hall»? | *In a clever, secret way, often to trick someone | Happily, with a big smile | Loudly and in front of everyone | Slowly and carefully
